The Dock and the app switcher show the program's name (or the name its launcher gives it), not "wine".

LaunchServices names a program that has no app bundle after its executable, so every Windows program
showed as "wine" in the Dock and in the app switcher, while its app menu already said "Quit ABZU"
(CrossOver Hack 13438 rewrites the loader's bundle name, from WINEPRELOADERAPPNAME or the program's
file name). This patch makes the Dock and the app switcher use that same name:

- dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m: just before a process becomes a Dock app (its first window), the Mac
  driver sets its LaunchServices display name to the bundle name, from inside the process, with
  LaunchServices' private _LSSetApplicationInformationItem and _kLSDisplayNameKey. Firefox
  (ipc/glue/ProcessUtils_mac.mm) and WebKit (WebProcessCocoa.mm) use this call today; Chromium used it
  for its helper processes until 2013. The symbols are looked up at run time with dlsym, as the driver
  already does for IOKit's private display calls: on a macOS without them nothing happens and the name
  stays "wine". A failed call is logged on the error channel.
- loader/main.c: WINEPRELOADERAPPNAME is no longer removed from the environment once read, so every
  process the program starts takes the same name. A game whose launcher starts the real game keeps its
  name in the Dock. Wine's own background programs inherit it too; they show no window, so no Dock
  icon. CrossOver's CW HACK 22144 still sets a per-program name when it runs (only with WINEDLLPATH set).
- loader/main.c: the name is written into the Info.plist's XML with '&', '<' and '>' escaped, and cut
  only between whole characters and escapes. Hack 13438 wrote it raw and cut it by bytes: a title such
  as "Ratchet & Clank", or a file name cut inside a UTF-8 character, made Core Foundation reject the
  whole Info.plist (plutil: "Encountered unknown ampersand-escape sequence", "Unable to convert string
  to correct encoding"), losing the name, the bundle identifier and LSUIElement. The slot stays 32
  bytes, so an escaped '&' takes 5 of them.

Upstream status: not offered yet. WineHQ's loader has no bundle-name rewrite (its CFBundleName is
"Wine"), so the WineHQ offer takes the name from the program's file name in the Mac driver; the
loader parts change CrossOver's own hack and go to CodeWeavers. The display-name call is private,
which WineHQ may decline.

